Erysipelas
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Fiery red plaque, Sharply demarcated raised border, Abrupt onset with fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-017
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of high fever, rigors, and a rapidly expanding, fiery red, painful facial rash.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden onset of high fever and rigors, followed rapidly by an intensely painful, hot, and swollen red rash spreading across the face. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate this from orbital cellulitis or necrotizing fasciitis, and to initiate immediate high-dose antibiotic therapy to prevent cavernous sinus thrombosis or systemic sepsis, which could not wait until the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up freezing and shaking, and an hour later my face felt like it was burning. It hurts so much, and the redness is spreading toward my eyes."
History of Present Illness: The patient was in their usual state of health until 8 hours ago, when they experienced an abrupt onset of high fever, chills, and rigors. Shortly after, they noted the rapid development of a fiery red, intensely erythematous skin plaque on the right cheek. The plaque is exquisitely painful, hot to the touch, and features sharply raised and well-demarcated advancing borders. The rash has crossed the nasal bridge to involve the left cheek in a classic butterfly distribution. The patient describes a feeling of intense tightness due to the rapid swelling. No recent facial trauma, dental infections, or sinus issues.
Functional Status: Independent, currently prostrate in bed due to systemic toxicity.
Social/Safety Concerns: Rapid systemic deterioration; proximity of infection to the orbits and central nervous system.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 118 | Temp: 39.2 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Systemically unwell, flushed, diaphoretic, experiencing rigors during examination.
Physical Exam: The face demonstrates a striking, fiery red plaque spanning the bilateral cheeks and nasal bridge. The advancing edges of the rash are sharply demarcated and distinctly raised above the surrounding normal skin. There is intense localized heat and exquisite tenderness to light palpation. A prominent peau d'orange appearance is visible on the cheeks due to severe superficial lymphatic oedema. There is tender regional lymphadenopathy in the bilateral submandibular and preauricular chains. Crucially, the extraocular movements are fully intact, there is no proptosis, and visual acuity is unaffected. No flaccid bullae or crepitus are present.
Environmental Observations: Patient in bed with multiple blankets due to chills.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications. No known drug allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Erysipelas (Facial) - Status: Acute, high acuity
Differentials:

Orbital Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact extraocular movements, lack of proptosis, and absence of pain with eye movement. The infection is currently confined to the superficial dermal lymphatics.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Acute Malar Rash) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute systemic toxicity (high fever, rigors), extreme pain, and the distinctly raised, sharply demarcated borders (malar rash is typically flat, non-tender, and lacks acute fever).

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: High fever, rigors, extreme pain, and unilateral-to-bilateral spread; contact dermatitis is intensely pruritic rather than severely painful, and lacks a febrile prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (due to systemic toxicity and anatomical location)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of a fiery red, raised facial plaque, sharply demarcated borders, and acute systemic toxicity, pathognomonic for facial erysipelas (superficial cellulitis typically caused by Group A Streptococcus). Triage accurately flagged the high fever and rapidly spreading facial rash as a high-acuity medical emergency. Immediate out-of-hours on-site assessment was essential to explicitly rule out orbital involvement (which would mandate emergency admission) and to instantly administer the first dose of targeted systemic antibiotics. Initiating aggressive oral therapy immediately, while setting a very low threshold for hospital transfer if deterioration occurred, effectively managed the acute sepsis risk in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg stat (covering typical streptococcal etiology and potential staphylococcal overlap). Prescribed Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 10 days. Administered Paracetamol 1000mg and Ibuprofen 400mg for fever and severe local pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your antibiotics precisely every 12 hours without missing a dose, you will maintain a lethal level of medicine in your blood, rapidly halting the bacterial spread and protecting your eyes. By keeping your head elevated on three pillows continuously, you will allow gravity to drain the severe lymphatic swelling, which will rapidly reduce the agonizing tightness in your face.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 24 hours to ensure the advancing border has arrested and the fever is subsiding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness spreads to involve your eyelids, if you experience double vision or pain when moving your eyes, if you become confused, or if the fever does not improve within 12 hours, your spouse must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Facial infections are dynamic and can progress quickly. IV antibiotics in the hospital may be required if oral medications fail.
Clinical Course: The severe chills and high fever should resolve within 24 hours of starting the antibiotics. The facial redness and swelling will take several days to slowly fade and may desquamate (peel) as it heals.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse must monitor the patient overnight. Immediate emergency transport is required for any visual changes, confusion, or inability to keep the oral antibiotics down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A sudden, aggressive bacterial infection has invaded the superficial layers of your facial skin, triggering a massive immune response and high fever. The antibiotics will rapidly kill this bacteria, but keeping your head elevated is critical to drain the extreme swelling.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire course of antibiotics. Rest strictly with your head elevated.

